    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `draft_reply` in `app/drafts.py`, and all 5 tests pass (the 2 existing ones plus 3 new ones).\n\nIt sends the request the same way `suggest_tag` does: model `DRAFT_MODEL`, `STYLE_GUIDE` as the instructions, and `\"{subject}\\n\\n{body}\"` as the input. It caps output at `max_output_tokens=MAX_DRAFT_TOKENS`, sets `store=False`, and passes `metadata={\"ticket_id\": ticket.id}`.\n\nThe returned `Draft` has the ticket id, `text` set to `response.output_text.strip()`, and `truncated` set to True only when the response's status is `\"incomplete\"` and `incomplete_details.reason` is `\"max_output_tokens\"`. A response cut short for another reason, such as `content_filter`, is not marked as truncated. I checked the installed openai 3.19.2 package to confirm the fields and parameter names.\n\nI also added `tests/test_drafts.py`, which uses a fake client like `tests/test_tags.py` does. It checks:\n- the request is sent with the right parameters;\n- whitespace is stripped from the reply;\n- a reply cut off at the token cap is marked truncated;\n- a reply stopped for any other reason is not.\n\n**Edge case:** `gpt-5.5` reasoning tokens count toward `max_output_tokens`. With a 400-token cap, a response can use up the budget on reasoning and return empty text with `truncated=True`. The code reports that correctly, but callers may want to handle it."
  },
